/* Expected output: 20
 * Why the optimizer runs to a FIXED POINT.  This is the listing from class
 * activity 4.  Compare:
 *     ./minicompiler tests/t4_14_fixed_point.cm out.s -onepass
 *     ./minicompiler tests/t4_14_fixed_point.cm out.s
 * One pass leaves 12 TAC instructions: it folds and propagates, simplifies
 * the branch, and removes print(999), but `print(k)` sits behind the label
 * L0, where the optimizer has to forget that k is 20.  The label only goes
 * away once the goto in front of it is gone, which takes further passes.
 * Running to a fixed point (5 passes) leaves 7, and the program is just
 * print(20). */
int main() {
    int n;
    int m;
    int k;
    n = 4 * 5;
    m = n + 0;
    k = m * 1;
    if (7 - 7) {
        print(999);
    }
    print(k);
    return 0;
}
